  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Extracting Square Roots by Factorization and Long Division",
    "description": "Find exact square roots by pairing prime factors or extracting digits in pairs.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the idea, derive the method, practice transfer, and use targeted remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5 “Square Roots and Cube Roots,” printed pages 180–205. Approved scope: Teach two exact methods for non-negative integer perfect squares. In prime factorization, pair equal prime factors and take one factor from each pair; explain why odd exponents prevent an integer square root. In the digit-pair long-division method, group digits from the units place, choose the largest trial digit at each stage, subtract, bring down the next pair, and verify by squaring. Work through multi-digit examples at the chapter's level, including interpreting the remainder and when the method gives an approximation for a non-square. Learners should choose a suitable method, keep place values aligned, and check the result by squaring."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Prime factorization and digit-pair extraction are two exact methods for square roots. Pair factors in one method and build the quotient digit by digit in the other.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Factor the radicand and pair equal primes. For 1,764=2²×3²×7², take one prime from each pair: √1,764=2×3×7=42. Any odd prime exponent shows that the number is not a perfect-square integer.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
